1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Hello everybody, thanks for watching this presentation.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,600
This presentation covers the topics for chapter 4, but with a lot of extra detail about how

3
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:25,840
to write numerically precise code in the context that come up in deep learning.

4
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,640
For people who are watching this on the book website, you should understand that I'm going

5
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:35,560
to be taking live questions from AI with the best audience members periodically, and I'll

6
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:41,760
read the questions as I answer them.

7
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,600
So I'm going to start out by looking at the slides, but I'm going to tab back and forth

8
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:46,960
periodically to check for questions from the audience.

9
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:53,120
So go ahead and ask questions as they come up, and I'll try to answer them quickly.

10
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:57,240
Today's topic is numerical computation for deep learning.

11
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:02,040
Basic idea of numerical computation is that we need to be very careful about the way that

12
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:05,840
we handle real numbers in a few different contexts.

13
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:13,640
One is if we use an algorithm that has to do some kind of, you know, careful representation

14
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,840
of real numbers with a finite number of bits.

15
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:22,040
Real numbers like pi have infinitely many digits, and then when we represent them in

16
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:27,160
a computer, we usually use 32-bit floating point numbers as an approximation for real

17
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:31,480
numbers when we implement a deep learning algorithm on GPU.

18
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:36,680
The use of 32 bits to represent something that really has infinitely many digits cannot

19
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:41,480
often cause errors, and we'd like to try to minimize the amount of damage to our program

20
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:45,040
that happens when we use that representation.

21
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:51,720
Another consideration for numerical computation is how sensitive a function is to very small

22
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:53,540
changes in its input.

23
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:59,280
This comes up even if we were able to theoretically use real numbers without having to represent

24
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,920
them in 32 bits.

25
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:05,420
When we do things like optimize a function, if that function is very sensitive to little

26
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:10,160
tiny changes in the input, it becomes a lot harder to search for the inputs that minimize

27
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:15,920
that function because little tiny changes in our search can strongly influence the outcome

28
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,600
of the search.

29
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,520
As a brief roadmap for today, I'm going to cover two different topics.

30
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:26,600
The first is iterative optimization, how we actually make algorithms that search for the

31
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:32,040
minimal point of some function, and how this gets affected by numerical considerations.

32
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:38,680
Next, I'm going to talk about rounding error underflow and overflow, how the use of 32

33
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:43,480
bits to represent real numbers can affect deep learning programs.

34
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:46,800
Starting out with iterative optimization.

35
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,560
We're going to cover three different topics here.

36
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:51,440
The first is how the gradient descent algorithm works.

37
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:56,600
The second is curvature, the idea that the functions we're optimizing are nonlinear,

38
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:02,520
and how the second order curvature of these functions affects the optimization process.

39
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:06,760
And then finally, we'll have a few comments on constrained optimization.

40
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:12,080
The basic algorithm that we use for most iterative optimization is gradient descent.

41
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:17,880
And I've illustrated this here with a really simple function, f of x equals one half x

42
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:18,880
squared.

43
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:23,640
You can see it plotted with the dashed blue line that I'm highlighting with my mouse cursor.

44
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:27,720
If we would like to minimize this function, we can do it by following the gradient.

45
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,040
Let's say that x is greater than zero.

46
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:35,400
The gradient in this case, because the function is one dimensional, is just the derivative.

47
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,600
So the derivative can be represented by the value f prime of x.

48
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:43,840
Over here, f prime of x, represented by the green line, is positive.

49
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,800
So if we descend the gradient by moving in the opposite direction of the derivative,

50
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:52,640
everywhere that we're over here where the green line is positive, gradient descent will

51
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:54,360
move left and downhill.

52
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:58,720
So starting here, we would trace the blue curve leftward until we reach its minimum

53
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:00,440
point here.

54
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:05,600
Where the derivative is zero, we are no longer told by the derivative to move in any particular

55
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:06,600
direction.

56
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:08,920
So we stop.

57
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:13,680
If we started over here on the left side, over here, the derivative is negative.

58
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,960
And we move in opposite the direction of the derivative.

59
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:20,280
So we would actually apply positive updates to our input x.

60
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:25,360
And we move rightward along the function x squared until we reach the minimum and stop

61
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,680
where the derivative is equal to zero.

62
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:33,280
I've described gradient descent basically in terms of this idea that we move downhill

63
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:36,560
until we find a point where the derivative is zero.

64
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:42,480
In the example of f of x equals 1 half x squared, there was only one point like that.

65
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:48,120
In reality, there might be a lot of different points that have zero derivatives.

66
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:54,120
Here I've drawn a function that's very squiggly and has several different local minima.

67
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:58,800
Local minima are points that are smaller in f of x value than all of the other points

68
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:04,280
surrounding them, but they're not necessarily the lowest point overall.

69
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:07,840
So here if we look to the left or the right of this point, we go uphill a little bit.

70
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:13,280
If you go further to the left, we can find much lower points.

71
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:17,400
Usually in the context of deep learning, we can't really expect to find this global minimum

72
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:18,400
over here.

73
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:21,800
We might want to do that, but it's probably not feasible.

74
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:25,360
In practice, we often give up and find a point more like this one here.

75
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,600
It's a local minimum, but the value of f of x is actually very low.

76
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:33,440
So we've done a good job of reducing the value of the function, even if we don't find our

77
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,840
way all the way to the bottom of the function.

78
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:41,440
In practice, we don't even necessarily find a local minimum.

79
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:47,200
In practice, we often just sort of get stuck for numerical reasons, and that's the topic

80
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:48,880
of this chapter.

81
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:52,920
I'm fast-forwarding a little bit, and I'm showing you a figure from chapter 8.

82
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:58,400
On the x-axis, I show you how long gradient descent has been running on a particular problem.

83
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:00,400
In the two different plots, I show you two different things.

84
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:06,320
On the left side, I show you how big the gradients are over time, specifically, I'm plotting

85
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:08,160
the norm of the gradient.

86
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:13,320
Every little black dot is a different step of the gradient descent algorithm.

87
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:16,240
So you can see it's kind of noisy on different steps.

88
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:19,760
It's not like we always see the same gradient norm twice in a row.

89
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:25,360
It bounces around a lot, but I've fit a smooth blue curve to these lines.

90
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:30,080
You can see how the gradient norm actually gradually gets bigger over time.

91
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:33,040
Based on the plots that I showed you on the previous slides, you might expect that the

92
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:36,480
gradient norm would shrink as you approach a local minimum, and it can register go down

93
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:37,480
to zero.

94
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:42,760
Well, it turns out that a lot of the time, we actually just get stuck and can't really

95
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:48,160
keep making progress toward regions of smaller gradient norm, and so we stop when f of x

96
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:53,840
has become very small, rather than when the gradient has gone away.

97
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,600
You could say maybe this plot that I made here, where I show the gradient getting bigger

98
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:58,960
over time, maybe that's an example of failure.

99
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,800
Maybe the algorithm is just broken, but actually, if you look at the plot on the right, I'm

100
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,760
showing you the error rate of a classifier that we're training with gradient descent,

101
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:12,080
and this error rate decreases over time successfully and levels off to about state-of-the-art

102
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:14,720
accuracy for the problem that this is training on.

103
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:18,560
So you can see that this is actually an example of the optimization algorithm succeeding from

104
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:19,920
our point of view.

105
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:25,000
It's not actually finding a local minimum, it's just finding a very low value of the

106
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,920
function f of x that we're minimizing, and that low value turns out to be good enough

107
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:32,640
for us to use the model.

108
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:35,880
Someone has asked, why don't we test a larger diversity when we reach the local minimum?

109
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:39,040
Wouldn't that tell us we could go lower elsewhere?

110
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:44,720
And the answer is yes, a lot of the time we do things like run multiple random restarts

111
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:50,600
of the optimization algorithm, and sometimes you can find lower values that way.

112
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:54,640
For a lot of problems, you also find that several different random restarts end up with

113
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:59,600
about the same final value.

114
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:02,720
Someone else asked, can you please explain gradient norm a bit?

115
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:09,880
The basic idea is if you have an input to your function x with multiple coordinates,

116
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,520
then the gradient gives you the derivative of your function with respect to each of those

117
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:17,920
coordinates, and that tells us the direction we should go in to move downhill.

118
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:22,960
If you measure the norm of that gradient, that gives you an idea of how steep the function

119
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:23,960
is.

120
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:29,240
It tells you how quickly you can expect the function to drop if you move in a specific

121
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:32,880
direction.

122
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:35,520
I'm going to skip the next question because it's answered later in the talk, and then

123
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:38,720
there's one more question about how can the gradient increase when you are using a descent

124
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:39,840
direction?

125
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:40,960
Is it not convex?

126
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:47,480
And it turns out that the gradient can increase if you optimize a neural net because the function

127
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:48,960
is not convex.

128
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:53,120
It's also possible that if you use too large of a step size, the gradient can increase

129
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:57,480
because you move to a region that's steeper.

130
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:03,040
I think of the way that we optimize deep learning functions as more of a way of life

131
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:07,360
than a strictly mathematical discipline right now.

132
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:12,560
If you approach optimization from a really pure math point of view, you're usually looking

133
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:17,320
to do something like find literally the smallest value of some function f of x.

134
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:21,460
Or you might have some other formally defined criterion, like you're looking for a critical

135
00:09:21,460 --> 00:09:27,660
point of f of x, where the value is locally the smallest in some neighborhood.

136
00:09:27,660 --> 00:09:32,520
For deep learning, we usually have a more qualitative pragmatic approach, where our

137
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:36,080
goal is to just decrease the value of f of x a lot.

138
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:40,300
And we aren't necessarily hoping to actually find even a local minimum.

139
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:44,600
We just want to make the error rate go down until the error rate is low enough that our

140
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:48,720
model can be used for some engineering application.

141
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:52,660
Actually finding a critical point might be very difficult, and a lot of the critical

142
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:56,340
points that we find may not be what we want.

143
00:09:56,340 --> 00:10:01,380
The next topic in our overview of iterative optimization is the effective curvature on

144
00:10:01,380 --> 00:10:04,860
these optimization algorithms.

145
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:09,420
We've mentioned that gradient descent in theory will approach a critical point where the

146
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:11,620
gradient is equal to zero.

147
00:10:11,620 --> 00:10:15,860
Here I show you three different kinds of critical points in one dimension.

148
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:21,960
There can be a minimum where the function curves upward around the critical point, and

149
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:27,880
the critical point is thus lower in value than all of the surrounding points.

150
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:32,640
There can also be a local maximum where the function curves downward, so the critical

151
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:35,560
point is higher in value than all the surrounding points.

152
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:43,160
Or you can have saddle points where you actually are neither a minimum nor a maximum.

153
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:45,940
Saddle points make a little bit more sense in two dimensions.

154
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:50,220
You can think of it as there's one axis where the critical point is a local minimum, and

155
00:10:50,220 --> 00:10:55,980
another axis where the critical point is a local maximum.

156
00:10:55,980 --> 00:11:00,180
Local maximum are usually not much of a problem for optimization algorithms.

157
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:04,020
Saddle points can be a problem for iterative optimization algorithms.

158
00:11:04,020 --> 00:11:08,260
There's one algorithm called Newton's method, where the basic strategy is to solve for a

159
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:10,980
point where the gradient is equal to zero.

160
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:16,200
If you're able to solve that problem algebraically, you can jump to a solution right away.

161
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:21,960
Unfortunately, a solution in terms of Newton's method is not always actually a minimum.

162
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:26,080
If you have a function that has saddle points in it, these are also places where the gradient

163
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:34,360
is zero, but they are not really minima because they're maxima across some axes.

164
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:39,800
So algorithms like Newton's method will accidentally teleport onto saddle points and get stuck.

165
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:44,020
It turns out that for neural networks, there are a lot more saddle points than local minima.

166
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:48,460
Fortunately, saddle points are not a big problem for gradient descent.

167
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:52,900
Unless you initialize directly on the saddle point, gradient descent can escape the saddle

168
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:54,060
point.

169
00:11:54,060 --> 00:11:57,600
If you look at appendix C of a paper called Qualitatively Characterizing Neural Network

170
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:02,660
Optimization Problems, I have some math there where I show why gradient descent is able

171
00:12:02,660 --> 00:12:04,660
to escape.

172
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:09,180
We can characterize all of these different critical points by using curvature.

173
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:16,240
As I said, a local minimum is where the function curves up around the point and a local maximum

174
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:18,480
is where it curves down.

175
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:25,200
We can also think of this as a way of predicting how well gradient descent will perform.

176
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:30,200
Gradient descent basically says we're going to look at the slope of the function and we're

177
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:36,520
going to pretend the function is linear nearby our current x value.

178
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:39,940
And then we're going to move in a direction that will make our linear approximation go

179
00:12:39,940 --> 00:12:42,660
downhill.

180
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:47,500
The amount of actual payoff that you get from that depends on how curved the function is.

181
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:51,980
If your function is perfectly linear, if you make a linear approximation to the function,

182
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,860
then it will just overlay the function exactly.

183
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:59,780
So here you can see I've got this dashed green line overlaying the blue line.

184
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:04,260
If we take a little step according to the direction of the derivative, we're going to

185
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:10,360
decrease the function f of x by exactly the same amount that our linear function predicted.

186
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:13,880
And that means that these iterative search algorithms, where we always just take little

187
00:13:13,880 --> 00:13:18,280
steps moving downhill, are going to work really well regardless of the size of step that we

188
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:20,200
use.

189
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:24,800
If we have negative curvature, where the function curves downward as we move away from the current

190
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:29,080
point, then we're actually in a very nice situation where if we take a step that was

191
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:36,340
predicted to reduce the dashed green linear function approximating f of x, we'll find

192
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:42,180
that f of x actually decreases even more than our approximation predicted it to do.

193
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:45,700
So if we can find directions of negative curvature, they're a good way to reduce the value of

194
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:47,340
the function very quickly.

195
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:52,500
Unfortunately, what happens a lot more often is we have directions of positive curvature.

196
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:56,820
With directions of positive curvature, the function curves up relative to the linear

197
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:57,820
function.

198
00:13:57,820 --> 00:14:02,800
With these small steps, we end up not nearly decreasing the function as much as we wanted

199
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:03,800
to.

200
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:09,040
In fact, if we take too big of a step, we can accidentally increase the function value.

201
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:13,840
We can overshoot the minimum in this direction and go back uphill.

202
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:17,000
That's where we start looking at what's called the numerics of the function.

203
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,240
We look at how much curvature there is to this function and see how it affects our

204
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:28,700
ability to take large steps to search for the minimum value of the function.

205
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:31,660
Someone asked what was the name of the paper that I just told.

206
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:33,060
You can actually see that in the slides.

207
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:36,380
They're available at deeplearningbook.org.

208
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:39,980
So far, I've shown you these one-dimensional examples of curvature.

209
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:43,220
But in practice, x is an n-dimensional vector.

210
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:49,260
And we need to understand how the curvature affects the function in many different directions.

211
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:51,920
We can do that using the Hessian matrix.

212
00:14:51,920 --> 00:14:57,880
The Hessian matrix is a matrix containing all of the second derivatives.

213
00:14:57,880 --> 00:15:03,640
If you look at row i and column j of the Hessian matrix, you'll find the derivative where

214
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:08,440
first we take the derivative with respect to xi, and next we take the derivative with

215
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,880
respect to xj.

216
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:15,400
It turns out that you can use the Hessian matrix to compute the second derivative in

217
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:17,760
a particular direction, d.

218
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:24,260
You can do that by computing d transpose hd, where d is a unit vector describing the direction

219
00:15:24,260 --> 00:15:27,260
where you would like to get the second derivative.

220
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:32,620
And it turns out that there's actually some nice intuition for what second derivative

221
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:34,460
you'll get.

222
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:39,660
Hessian matrices are usually symmetric, assuming that different properties of the function

223
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:40,660
are held.

224
00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:47,100
And a symmetric real-valued matrix has an eigen decomposition in terms of orthogonal

225
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:50,880
eigenvectors and eigenvalues associated with each eigenvector.

226
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:56,080
So you can write all of the eigenvectors in a matrix q, and they become this orthogonal

227
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:58,600
basis for the space that you're in.

228
00:15:58,600 --> 00:16:01,720
And then you have a diagonal matrix of eigenvalues.

229
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:06,240
If we look back at some of the slides from chapter two, we can see what happens when

230
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:11,640
we multiply by a matrix that has particular eigenvectors and eigenvalues.

231
00:16:11,640 --> 00:16:16,760
If we start out with a bunch of points that are represented by this blue circle here and

232
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:24,020
we multiply each of these points by a matrix that has eigenvectors v1 and v2, we'll see

233
00:16:24,020 --> 00:16:29,860
that each of the points gets stretched away from the origin according to the eigenvalues

234
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:31,660
of the matrix.

235
00:16:31,660 --> 00:16:38,260
So if you have a large lambda one eigenvalue associated with v1, you'll actually stretch

236
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:41,660
out the circle in this direction.

237
00:16:41,660 --> 00:16:46,620
Points will get pulled further out by a factor of lambda one.

238
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:51,520
And then if you have a small eigenvalue associated with direction v2, points will get pulled

239
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:52,520
inward.

240
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:59,040
So you can see that these directions will be, when you multiply h by d, d will either

241
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:05,120
get stretched or shrunk according to how big or small the eigenvalues of the Hessian

242
00:17:05,120 --> 00:17:06,720
are in that direction.

243
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:11,720
And then when you take the dot product with the direction, again, you'll end up just measuring

244
00:17:11,720 --> 00:17:13,760
the size of that vector.

245
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:19,980
So you'll end up saying that the second derivative is based on how much the Hessian shrinks

246
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:25,300
or stretches your vector in that particular direction.

247
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:28,780
But what happens if your direction is not an eigenvector?

248
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:33,220
Well then it turns out that you basically interpolate based on the eigenvectors that

249
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:34,540
are nearby.

250
00:17:34,540 --> 00:17:39,580
You can compute the second derivative as a sum over all the different eigenvalues.

251
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:46,760
And you multiply by this term that measures how similar the direction you're looking in

252
00:17:46,760 --> 00:17:49,920
is to that corresponding eigenvalue.

253
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:53,600
You can kind of see that in this plot, that if you have a direction that's very closely

254
00:17:53,600 --> 00:18:00,800
aligned with eigenvector two, you're going to get your direction stretched by approximately

255
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:02,600
eigenvalue two.

256
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:09,040
And then as you move towards eigenvalue one, the amount that your direction gets stretched

257
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:15,860
is going to scale gradually up to the amount of stretching that comes from eigenvalue one.

258
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:18,780
You're never going to stretch more than the maximum eigenvalue.

259
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:23,580
You're never going to stretch less than the minimum eigenvalue.

260
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:29,380
We can use these eigenvalues to figure out what the optimal step size is likely to be.

261
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:34,660
So first off, if we have a direction of negative curvature, even a second order approximation

262
00:18:34,660 --> 00:18:37,900
would tell us that we can keep stepping forever.

263
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:42,960
That's probably not going to be true for a neural net because it's very nonlinear, it's

264
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,360
not even really quadratic.

265
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:48,800
It's likely to start going back uphill at some point for a large step size.

266
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:52,000
The main place where the Hessian tells us a lot of information is if we look at the

267
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,320
case of positive curvature, we know that if we start following the linear function downhill,

268
00:18:57,320 --> 00:18:59,120
we can decrease the value of the function.

269
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:02,480
But if we step too far, we'll start to go back uphill.

270
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:06,920
We don't want to step any further than this minimum point here.

271
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:12,100
We can make a second order Taylor series approximation to the value that we'll get when we take a

272
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:14,060
specific gradient step.

273
00:19:14,060 --> 00:19:19,540
We can say we're starting off at some point x0, and we're taking a gradient descent step

274
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:26,260
where we move by step size epsilon multiplied by a gradient g.

275
00:19:26,260 --> 00:19:32,980
The second order Taylor series approximation tells us that the value at this new point

276
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:39,360
will be approximately equal to the value at our current point minus epsilon times the

277
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,360
squared norm of the gradient.

278
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:49,040
This is the first order Taylor series term, plus one half epsilon squared times the gradient

279
00:19:49,040 --> 00:19:52,000
multiplied by the Hessian multiplied by the gradient.

280
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,160
This here is the directional second derivative in the direction of the gradient, but also

281
00:19:57,160 --> 00:19:59,440
scaled by the size of the gradient.

282
00:20:00,420 --> 00:20:06,260
So if we then solve this equation for the minimum predicted value that we'll get after

283
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:12,100
the step, and we solve it in terms of the step size, assuming that we're looking in

284
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:18,220
a direction of positive curvature, we find that the optimal step size is equal to the

285
00:20:18,220 --> 00:20:22,900
squared norm of the gradient divided by g transpose hg.

286
00:20:22,900 --> 00:20:24,980
So what does this tell us overall?

287
00:20:24,980 --> 00:20:29,300
The numerator says that if the gradient is bigger, we can take bigger steps.

288
00:20:29,300 --> 00:20:32,880
So big gradients will speed up the optimization algorithm.

289
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:38,200
In the denominator, we actually see that this g transpose hg term can shrink the optimal

290
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,680
step size a lot.

291
00:20:39,680 --> 00:20:44,480
So if the gradient happens to point in a direction that's aligned with an eigenvector of the

292
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:48,480
Hessian that has a big eigenvalue, you have to slow down a lot.

293
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:52,960
Otherwise we'll overshoot the minimum and go back uphill.

294
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:57,080
Overall this shows us something about why when we actually run an iterative search in

295
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:01,720
practice, we may not actually arrive at a critical point.

296
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:06,060
Over time, we'd expect the gradient to get smaller and smaller, but we'll often also

297
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:11,220
find for the kinds of optimization problems that come up with neural nets, we'll find

298
00:21:11,220 --> 00:21:16,300
that this g transpose hg term gets very big, and we're just not able to make very big steps

299
00:21:16,300 --> 00:21:21,140
anymore, even if the gradient remains large.

300
00:21:21,140 --> 00:21:23,960
We can measure this with something called the condition number.

301
00:21:23,960 --> 00:21:30,820
The condition number looks at the largest ratio we can find between two of our eigenvalues.

302
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:36,060
If there's a very large eigenvalue and a very small eigenvalue, it means that when our gradient

303
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:40,500
points in the direction of the large eigenvalue, we're forced to take very small steps.

304
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:44,380
And we have to do that even if sometimes the gradient would point in the direction of a

305
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:47,740
small eigenvalue, and it would be safe to take larger steps.

306
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:52,940
Basically, if there's a wide range of eigenvalues, gradient descent has to miss out on a lot

307
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:55,640
of the potential value that it could find on each step.

308
00:21:55,640 --> 00:22:00,880
It has to keep the step size small to avoid accidentally going up.

309
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:03,280
You can see this with this contour plot.

310
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:05,480
Here we're minimizing a quadratic function.

311
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:11,840
Each of the black rings shows a set of points where the function is of equal value.

312
00:22:11,840 --> 00:22:14,200
So at the middle here, we've got a minimum of the function.

313
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:19,680
Every time we see a ring on our way out, we're taking a step up in value of the function.

314
00:22:20,500 --> 00:22:23,700
The red path is showing how gradient descent moves.

315
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:28,580
Ideally, gradient descent would want to just point straight at the minimum and go straight

316
00:22:28,580 --> 00:22:29,580
there.

317
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:35,620
Instead, we keep finding that the gradients tend to point kind of across this canyon.

318
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:40,380
And so we keep going down the canyon wall, overshooting the bottom of the canyon, and

319
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:41,660
coming to the other side of the canyon.

320
00:22:41,660 --> 00:22:46,380
So you're going this zigzagging fashion where we're always walking up or down a canyon wall

321
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:51,040
instead of kind of flowing down along the bottom of the canyon.

322
00:22:51,040 --> 00:22:52,880
You can see this with neural networks.

323
00:22:52,880 --> 00:23:00,560
That same paper that I mentioned earlier includes this visualization of a little 3D representation

324
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:07,840
of what happens in a neural net optimization problem with millions of parameters.

325
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:11,120
We can see that toward the end of training, gradient descent as represented by this blue

326
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:18,020
path is moving down this really tight canyon with the green 3D mesh.

327
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:24,940
And so the gradient is actually still pretty large here, but the curvature is even larger

328
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:29,120
and the curvature makes it really hard to take large steps.

329
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:32,740
Some things about this visualization are a little bit hard to understand because when

330
00:23:32,740 --> 00:23:38,180
you work with multiple dimensions and project them down to just a few dimensions, some things

331
00:23:38,180 --> 00:23:39,180
can be lost.

332
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:45,440
So one thing that we can't see here is that this blue curve is actually zigzagging a lot,

333
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:47,840
just like in the previous visualization.

334
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:54,160
It's just that the way that we made this plot means that we can't actually see the other

335
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:56,600
dimensions where it's zigzagging.

336
00:23:56,600 --> 00:24:02,600
But those zigzags are stopping it from moving a lot faster along the bottom of this canyon.

337
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:08,400
The last topic that we'll cover for iterative optimization is the idea of constrained optimization.

338
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:12,020
But I don't actually have a lot to say about this, I'm just going to very quickly mention

339
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:14,980
a little bit about how it works.

340
00:24:14,980 --> 00:24:24,220
If you have constraints for a function, like if you have some values g that measure how

341
00:24:24,220 --> 00:24:30,980
much you deviate from exactly satisfying a constraint or some values h that measure how

342
00:24:30,980 --> 00:24:35,500
far you are on the wrong side of some inequality, you can actually take your original function

343
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:45,000
f of x and transform it into this new function called the Lagrangian function where you penalize

344
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:49,440
the function for violating those constraints.

345
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:55,560
And you can then solve instead of just a minimization problem, you can solve a min max problem where

346
00:24:55,560 --> 00:25:00,880
you want to keep increasing the coefficients on those penalty terms until you satisfy the

347
00:25:00,880 --> 00:25:02,600
penalty terms.

348
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:06,780
This will give you a solution to the constrained optimization problem rather than just letting

349
00:25:06,780 --> 00:25:09,420
you search over all of space.

350
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:15,740
In this book, we usually use this Lagrangian or it's also called the KKT version when

351
00:25:15,740 --> 00:25:20,820
you include both the exact and the inequality constraints.

352
00:25:20,820 --> 00:25:26,020
In this book, we mostly use this approach for solving problems in theory, like if we

353
00:25:26,020 --> 00:25:30,780
want to show that the Gaussian is the probability distribution of the highest entropy, we can

354
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:35,360
use some of these penalty terms to constrain that the function we're searching over is

355
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:39,200
a probability distribution that's non-negative and sums to one.

356
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:43,000
In practice, if we actually want to write a program that we run where we use several

357
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,800
iterations to search for a solution, there's a much easier thing you can do.

358
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:51,080
Instead of having this min max problem and Lagrangian penalties and so on, you can just

359
00:25:51,080 --> 00:25:56,280
project x back to the nearest point that satisfies the constraints after each step.

360
00:25:56,300 --> 00:26:03,660
That's a very practical, easy to implement way of solving these kinds of problems.

361
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:08,740
Someone has asked, which data visualization did you use to plot the data points?

362
00:26:08,740 --> 00:26:10,300
For that, you should read the paper.

363
00:26:10,300 --> 00:26:13,540
It's a visualization that became up specifically for that paper.

364
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:17,540
It's not a standard technique.

365
00:26:17,540 --> 00:26:20,620
Someone else asked, what happens if we do not choose the direction where the gradient

366
00:26:20,620 --> 00:26:25,500
points, but select the direction where the step we can take is maximal?

367
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:30,160
You can actually look for directions where you intentionally seek out negative curvature

368
00:26:30,160 --> 00:26:31,600
and so on.

369
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:35,760
It turns out to be difficult to do that in high-dimensional space.

370
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:38,960
Someone asks, what gradient descent optimizes would you recommend?

371
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:44,040
I would say usually Adam or gradient descent with momentum are pretty good, and a lot of

372
00:26:44,040 --> 00:26:46,720
the time you should consider using gradient clipping.

373
00:26:46,720 --> 00:26:51,000
The next part of our overall roadmap is to discuss what happens when we have rounding

374
00:26:51,020 --> 00:26:55,820
error, when we have underflow, overflow, all of these issues that happen when we use a

375
00:26:55,820 --> 00:27:00,340
finite number of bits to represent real numbers.

376
00:27:00,340 --> 00:27:01,340
This is really important.

377
00:27:01,340 --> 00:27:06,180
I know it sounds kind of boring to talk about this low-level stuff with bits, but writing

378
00:27:06,180 --> 00:27:10,100
numerically precise code is actually a deep learning superscale.

379
00:27:10,100 --> 00:27:13,980
A lot of the time you'll find that deep learning algorithms sort of work when you code them

380
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:14,980
up.

381
00:27:14,980 --> 00:27:19,300
The loss will go down, the accuracy will go up, the accuracy will get within a few percentage

382
00:27:19,320 --> 00:27:21,320
points of the state of the art.

383
00:27:21,320 --> 00:27:25,560
You can check your code really carefully and you can find that there aren't really any

384
00:27:25,560 --> 00:27:31,000
bugs, but maybe there are some things that aren't quite optimal about it.

385
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:35,360
That's often caused by a loss of numerical precision, where you run your gradient descent

386
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:40,440
algorithm and you don't get quite as good as results as you think you should.

387
00:27:40,440 --> 00:27:43,960
Another time you'll find that your deep learning algorithm explodes.

388
00:27:43,960 --> 00:27:45,480
You get a lot of not-in-number values.

389
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:47,680
You get a lot of very large values.

390
00:27:47,700 --> 00:27:51,020
This might happen even if you make your learning rate relatively small and you think everything

391
00:27:51,020 --> 00:27:52,860
should be stable.

392
00:27:52,860 --> 00:27:57,740
Both of these problems actually usually come from writing code that doesn't have high enough

393
00:27:57,740 --> 00:28:00,140
numerical precision.

394
00:28:00,140 --> 00:28:04,940
Being able to write numerically precise code can really differentiate how well you perform

395
00:28:04,940 --> 00:28:07,060
as a deep learning practitioner.

396
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:09,860
A lot of people have heard the story of how when I first thought of generative adversarial

397
00:28:09,860 --> 00:28:14,860
nets, I was at a bar and I went home and I coded them up immediately.

398
00:28:14,880 --> 00:28:17,520
In an hour or so later, I actually had them working.

399
00:28:17,520 --> 00:28:22,480
A lot of people have wondered how I was able to do that, and part of the answer is that

400
00:28:22,480 --> 00:28:28,200
I knew about all of this really boring numerical precision stuff, so I didn't waste any time

401
00:28:28,200 --> 00:28:33,120
shooting myself in the foot writing numerically imprecise code.

402
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:37,720
That reduces the amount of time it takes to get the algorithm working a lot.

403
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:43,600
The basic idea is that when you use a finite number of bits to represent a real number,

404
00:28:43,620 --> 00:28:47,620
you get both rounding errors and what are called truncation errors.

405
00:28:47,620 --> 00:28:52,860
In a digital computer, we're usually talking about using float 32 or some other similar

406
00:28:52,860 --> 00:28:55,180
schemes to represent real numbers.

407
00:28:55,180 --> 00:28:59,380
That's especially true when we're using CUDA on GPUs.

408
00:28:59,380 --> 00:29:01,500
Sometimes we use other floating formats.

409
00:29:01,500 --> 00:29:05,820
A lot of the problems we're going to describe here will often mostly go away if you're able

410
00:29:05,820 --> 00:29:13,340
to switch to float 64, but that usually requires slower computation on GPU or Maven

411
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:14,720
4C to move to CPU.

412
00:29:14,720 --> 00:29:17,320
It will also force you to use more memory.

413
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:24,560
If you can write numerically precise code using float 32, then that can be a big advantage.

414
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:32,760
There's basically three categories of problems that happen on these kinds of 32-bit representations.

415
00:29:32,760 --> 00:29:36,560
First, most numbers that you work with, they're not going to cause catastrophic problems on

416
00:29:36,560 --> 00:29:38,920
their own, but they are going to get rounded.

417
00:29:38,920 --> 00:29:43,540
If you have some real number x and you turn it into a representation on computer, it's

418
00:29:43,540 --> 00:29:48,580
going to get rounded to some value x plus delta, where delta is just some small value

419
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:52,700
describing the rounding error that happened, that you'll be off by a little bit from what

420
00:29:52,700 --> 00:29:54,940
you meant to be.

421
00:29:54,940 --> 00:30:00,740
A larger problem is when you have some extremely large value of x, you just can't represent

422
00:30:00,740 --> 00:30:06,100
it with the range of numbers that your float 32 values can represent.

423
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:11,640
It will actually get replaced by some placeholder, like inf, just saying that x is too large

424
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:13,760
to represent.

425
00:30:13,760 --> 00:30:15,880
Now it's not really a finite value anymore.

426
00:30:15,880 --> 00:30:20,480
You've got this infinite number, and your code will probably still go ahead and keep

427
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:27,160
doing arithmetic on this number as if it were a real value, and then you'll get in trouble.

428
00:30:27,160 --> 00:30:31,360
There's another topic which is still basically a kind of rounding error, but it's a particularly

429
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:35,860
harmful kind of rounding error, where if you have a very small value of x, it can get

430
00:30:35,860 --> 00:30:38,140
replaced by zero.

431
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:42,060
This is especially harmful because a lot of the algorithms in deep learning often assume

432
00:30:42,060 --> 00:30:46,540
that we have some very small but non-zero variable.

433
00:30:46,540 --> 00:30:52,540
For example, if you use the logistic sigmoid activation function, you can pass very negative

434
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:58,620
values to the logistic sigmoid, and algebraically, they'll never become quite zero, but in a

435
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:03,560
computer, they can definitely get rounded down to zero.

436
00:31:03,560 --> 00:31:06,600
People are asking, aren't these numerical issues already addressed in the current deep

437
00:31:06,600 --> 00:31:09,160
learning libraries?

438
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:13,000
The answer is basically yes, but you need to know when to look out for them.

439
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:18,040
A lot of the time, you write your own code, and you might end up rewriting a version of

440
00:31:18,040 --> 00:31:22,280
something that already has a numerically stable variant.

441
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:27,040
Sometimes you might need to, for example, compute a softmax over several tensors instead

442
00:31:27,060 --> 00:31:31,460
of just one tensor, and you'll need to know how to do that in a numerically stable way

443
00:31:31,460 --> 00:31:37,380
if your library only provides a softmax for a single tensor.

444
00:31:37,380 --> 00:31:42,380
Some other people are asking if there's any pointers on the best way to deal with NANDs.

445
00:31:42,380 --> 00:31:45,180
That's basically what the next several slides will do.

446
00:31:45,180 --> 00:31:49,140
Someone asks, how does the 64-bit representation solve the NAND error?

447
00:31:49,140 --> 00:31:52,380
The basic idea is that if you have 32 bits, there's a certain range of values that you

448
00:31:52,380 --> 00:31:55,260
can represent.

449
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:59,920
If you have 64 bits, that range expands.

450
00:31:59,920 --> 00:32:05,840
So a program that might cause a NAND when you use 32 bits might actually avoid the NAND

451
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:08,000
when you use 64 bits.

452
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:11,840
If you use a program that uses even bigger numbers, you can eventually still get NANDs

453
00:32:11,840 --> 00:32:13,040
for 64 bits.

454
00:32:13,040 --> 00:32:19,760
It's just that fewer programs cause problems using 64 bits than using 32.

455
00:32:19,780 --> 00:32:25,260
One example is, if you look at the PyLin 2 repository that I used to work on in grad

456
00:32:25,260 --> 00:32:29,740
school before we had TensorFlow, you can look at some of the unit tests.

457
00:32:29,740 --> 00:32:35,540
And when I compute the KL divergence between two Gaussian distributions, if I use float64,

458
00:32:35,540 --> 00:32:41,420
the unit test expects the KL divergence to have a minimum value of zero.

459
00:32:41,420 --> 00:32:48,020
But if I use float32, the unit test actually has to allow the KL divergence to go slightly

460
00:32:48,020 --> 00:32:49,660
negative.

461
00:32:49,660 --> 00:32:54,640
So one example of how we can get rounding errors and they can affect this code is what

462
00:32:54,640 --> 00:32:57,720
happens when you add a very small number to a larger number.

463
00:32:57,720 --> 00:33:02,120
So we're going to make an array using 32-bit floats, and we're going to store the value

464
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:06,000
zero and the value 10 to the minus eight in this array.

465
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:11,760
If we compute the argmax of this array, the argmax is one because position zero has a

466
00:33:11,760 --> 00:33:18,040
value of zero, position one has a positive but very small value of 10 to the minus eight.

467
00:33:18,040 --> 00:33:21,940
So now suppose that we take these numbers and we add them to a much larger number.

468
00:33:21,940 --> 00:33:23,420
We add them to one.

469
00:33:23,420 --> 00:33:29,100
Well, zero is going to have one added to it and end up with a value of one.

470
00:33:29,100 --> 00:33:36,060
Ideally, one plus 10 to the minus eight would be represented as one, but, sorry, one, one

471
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:40,220
point, you know, zero, zero, zero, zero, zero, one after the correct number of zeros.

472
00:33:40,220 --> 00:33:44,860
In this case, it's actually going to get rounded to one, which isn't quite what we want.

473
00:33:44,860 --> 00:33:48,280
When you take the argmax of the new array where we added one to it, we actually get

474
00:33:48,280 --> 00:33:53,880
that the argmax is zero now because NumPy breaks ties to the first index that's tied

475
00:33:53,880 --> 00:33:55,880
for the max.

476
00:33:55,880 --> 00:34:01,440
So this addition of a number that caused a rounding error has actually downstream changed

477
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:03,080
the results quite a lot.

478
00:34:03,080 --> 00:34:07,440
We only really wanted to extract a single bit from this computation, and we're now actually

479
00:34:07,440 --> 00:34:11,240
getting the value of that bit wrong.

480
00:34:11,240 --> 00:34:16,180
There's some secondary effects that happen when we have rounding error and overflow and

481
00:34:16,180 --> 00:34:17,820
underflow errors.

482
00:34:17,820 --> 00:34:21,340
What happens is after the first error, we do some more math, and then the second error

483
00:34:21,340 --> 00:34:24,060
causes a bigger problem.

484
00:34:24,060 --> 00:34:28,180
So for example, say we have some code that computes x minus y.

485
00:34:28,180 --> 00:34:32,540
X minus y is a relatively safe thing to do to start with.

486
00:34:32,540 --> 00:34:35,500
There's definitely some ways that can go wrong, but there's not anything fundamentally wrong

487
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:37,380
with doing a subtraction.

488
00:34:37,380 --> 00:34:42,680
And now suppose that x overflows to nth, y overflows to nth, well then we've got infinity

489
00:34:42,680 --> 00:34:47,480
minus infinity, and we don't really know which infinity is larger.

490
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:49,920
This kind of operation is not really defined.

491
00:34:49,920 --> 00:34:53,720
So now our x minus y will turn these nths into nanths.

492
00:34:53,720 --> 00:34:58,880
A lot of the time when you have overflow, the way that you first see it is that you'll

493
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:01,960
get a nan in your loss function or your gradient.

494
00:35:01,960 --> 00:35:06,200
So somewhere in your computational graph, you'll get an nth, and then by the time it

495
00:35:06,200 --> 00:35:10,700
actually gets printed out or plotted somewhere, it will have been turned into a nan by these

496
00:35:10,700 --> 00:35:14,700
additional steps of arithmetic that came afterward.

497
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:18,860
One of the main functions that's often the culprit of numerical precision problems in

498
00:35:18,860 --> 00:35:27,860
deep learning programs is the x function that computes the natural logarithms base e raised

499
00:35:27,860 --> 00:35:29,620
to the power of x.

500
00:35:29,620 --> 00:35:32,140
This will overflow for large values of x.

501
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:36,880
And a lot of the time when I say large values, I am talking about extremely large values.

502
00:35:36,880 --> 00:35:40,480
For x, when I say large values, I'm actually talking about numbers that are not very big.

503
00:35:40,480 --> 00:35:47,680
If you're using float 32, a value of 89 past 2x will overflow to nth.

504
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:52,440
To make sure that your program doesn't have trouble with x, just never use large x as

505
00:35:52,440 --> 00:35:54,360
the argument for x.

506
00:35:54,360 --> 00:35:59,280
And make sure that you design the structure of your functions that you compute so that

507
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:02,580
they will never actually pass a large x to x.

508
00:36:02,580 --> 00:36:06,700
A lot of the rest of this lecture will be about ideas for how you can prevent that from

509
00:36:06,700 --> 00:36:10,340
happening in specific cases.

510
00:36:10,340 --> 00:36:16,380
The other big problem with x is that if your argument to it is very negative, x of x can

511
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:17,380
underflow.

512
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:18,900
This may not be a problem.

513
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:24,380
If you think about it, x of a very negative number is just some small number epsilon.

514
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:29,440
And if you round that number down to zero, you've only made a mistake of size epsilon.

515
00:36:29,440 --> 00:36:33,640
So if that was your final output of the function, it's not a very big error.

516
00:36:33,640 --> 00:36:39,480
Unfortunately, if you then do other math on top of this output, rounding a small number

517
00:36:39,480 --> 00:36:42,200
epsilon down to zero can be catastrophic.

518
00:36:42,200 --> 00:36:47,440
If you're using x of x in a denominator or as an argument to a logarithm and so on,

519
00:36:47,440 --> 00:36:51,000
it can cause really big problems.

520
00:36:51,020 --> 00:36:56,260
Another case where you can sometimes encounter danger is when you have subtraction, especially

521
00:36:56,260 --> 00:37:02,860
if you want to guarantee that the output of the subtraction is going to be positive.

522
00:37:02,860 --> 00:37:07,780
So if you have two values, x and y, and you know algebraically that you can prove that

523
00:37:07,780 --> 00:37:12,580
x is always greater than y, in a computer, you may actually find that x minus y ends

524
00:37:12,580 --> 00:37:14,060
up being negative.

525
00:37:14,060 --> 00:37:18,900
So if you rely on x minus y being positive in subsequent steps of your program, you

526
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:20,500
could run into trouble.

527
00:37:20,500 --> 00:37:23,840
One case where we see this a lot is computing the variance.

528
00:37:23,840 --> 00:37:28,300
So there's one way that you can write the variance, where it's the expectation of a

529
00:37:28,300 --> 00:37:30,080
squared value.

530
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:34,000
If you implement the variance this way in your code, it's always going to give you positive

531
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:39,720
numbers because the output of the squaring value will always be non-negative.

532
00:37:39,720 --> 00:37:43,560
I shouldn't say positive, sorry.

533
00:37:43,560 --> 00:37:49,360
And then if you look at the difference between the expectation of the squared value of the

534
00:37:49,380 --> 00:37:55,700
function and the square of the expected value of the function, algebraically that's a perfectly

535
00:37:55,700 --> 00:37:58,940
valid way of rearranging this expression for the variance.

536
00:37:58,940 --> 00:38:03,060
And a lot of times this is actually the one where we actually have access to those values

537
00:38:03,060 --> 00:38:04,620
to compute it.

538
00:38:04,620 --> 00:38:08,780
Unfortunately, it's numerically unstable because if we have a little bit of rounding error

539
00:38:08,780 --> 00:38:13,180
on the left and a little bit of rounding error on the right, and these values were very close

540
00:38:13,180 --> 00:38:16,820
to zero to start with, if the rounding error goes in the wrong direction, we can end up

541
00:38:16,840 --> 00:38:20,560
with a small negative value instead of zero.

542
00:38:20,560 --> 00:38:24,600
And that can cause trouble, especially if you were planning to pass this value to a square

543
00:38:24,600 --> 00:38:29,640
root or a logarithm later on.

544
00:38:29,640 --> 00:38:33,480
Logarithm and square root are also functions where you want to be very careful.

545
00:38:33,480 --> 00:38:38,320
The logarithm of zero is negative infinity, and the logarithm of a negative number is

546
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:39,560
imaginary.

547
00:38:39,560 --> 00:38:45,240
Usually in software frameworks like NumPy, we end up treating that as a not a number.

548
00:38:45,260 --> 00:38:49,220
The square root of zero is zero, so that's actually safe, but the derivative of square

549
00:38:49,220 --> 00:38:54,580
root has a divide by zero if you take the derivative at x equals zero.

550
00:38:54,580 --> 00:38:59,540
So you definitely want to avoid underflow or rounding to a negative value in the argument

551
00:38:59,540 --> 00:39:01,820
to either log or square root.

552
00:39:01,820 --> 00:39:05,860
One very common place where this comes up is when we compute the standard deviation.

553
00:39:05,860 --> 00:39:08,580
The standard deviation is the square root of the variance.

554
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:13,180
So if you compute the variance using the dangerous strategy from this slide, you can end up with

555
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:16,960
this rounding to a small negative number, and then you take the square root of that small

556
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:20,360
negative number and run into trouble.

557
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:25,440
The logarithm of x is a common pattern that comes up a lot in deep learning.

558
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,800
We often see this when we're computing log probabilities, and the probability is based

559
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:31,880
on taking the x of some argument.

560
00:39:31,880 --> 00:39:35,880
Whenever you see this, you should simplify it to just the value x.

561
00:39:35,880 --> 00:39:40,440
Theona will do this for you automatically, a lot of frameworks don't.

562
00:39:40,620 --> 00:39:46,900
If you do this, you can avoid overflowing the x, because you just take x rather than

563
00:39:46,900 --> 00:39:49,740
competing x of x first before competing the log.

564
00:39:49,740 --> 00:39:56,740
You can also avoid underflow in x, which would then cause the logarithm to turn into negative.

565
00:39:59,780 --> 00:40:02,420
So now I have a little bit of an exercise for you.

566
00:40:02,420 --> 00:40:07,820
Let's say that we're doing a little bit of a hack to avoid a divide by zero.

567
00:40:07,820 --> 00:40:14,820
We want to compute a normalized version of x, where x will have unit standard deviation,

568
00:40:15,720 --> 00:40:21,320
and we divide x by some estimate of the standard deviation in order to do that.

569
00:40:21,320 --> 00:40:25,520
We know that our standard deviation might have some round to zero troubles, so we're

570
00:40:25,520 --> 00:40:31,520
going to add a small constant epsilon of 10 to the minus seven to our standard deviation

571
00:40:31,520 --> 00:40:35,360
in order to make sure we don't divide by zero when we compute the normalized version of

572
00:40:35,360 --> 00:40:37,720
x.

573
00:40:37,720 --> 00:40:39,740
So there's two different ways we can imagine doing this.

574
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:44,100
One is we could take the square root of epsilon plus the variance, and use that as our standard

575
00:40:44,100 --> 00:40:45,300
deviation.

576
00:40:45,300 --> 00:40:50,620
The other version of the hack is we could take epsilon plus the square root of the variance.

577
00:40:50,620 --> 00:40:55,540
So which of these is the better way to implement this hack?

578
00:40:55,540 --> 00:41:01,500
Does anybody want to answer on the chat?

579
00:41:01,500 --> 00:41:02,580
The first one.

580
00:41:02,580 --> 00:41:04,060
So why do you say the first one?

581
00:41:04,060 --> 00:41:06,860
Oh, you got to vote for the second one as well.

582
00:41:06,860 --> 00:41:15,400
So does somebody want to give a reason for one of their votes?

583
00:41:15,400 --> 00:41:18,440
So the square root of x will be too small.

584
00:41:18,440 --> 00:41:20,480
That can definitely happen.

585
00:41:20,480 --> 00:41:25,040
If your x is already just barely as big as you can represent and variance is zero, it

586
00:41:25,040 --> 00:41:28,920
can round down.

587
00:41:28,940 --> 00:41:37,540
There's also another issue here, which is think about what happens if the variance is

588
00:41:37,540 --> 00:41:44,100
already implemented safely, and we know that it won't get rounded down too low.

589
00:41:44,100 --> 00:41:48,340
Let's also suppose we make epsilon a little bit bigger, so if we divide by a square root

590
00:41:48,340 --> 00:41:50,860
of epsilon, that won't give us divide by zero.

591
00:41:50,860 --> 00:41:55,380
Is there any other problem that might come up here?

592
00:41:55,380 --> 00:41:58,660
So there is one other problem that might come up here.

593
00:41:58,660 --> 00:42:05,760
If we use this version, where we have the epsilon plus square root of the variance, we

594
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:12,600
can actually get, when we compute the derivative of normalized x later on, we can have this

595
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:18,800
problem that the square root of the variance might be evaluated exactly for the variance

596
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:24,720
being equal to zero, and when we take the derivative of square root, we actually get

597
00:42:24,720 --> 00:42:27,400
a divide by zero in that case.

598
00:42:27,400 --> 00:42:32,180
So usually you want to put the epsilon inside the square root, but you do need to make sure

599
00:42:32,180 --> 00:42:35,460
that your epsilon is big enough that it won't get rounded down to zero when you take the

600
00:42:35,460 --> 00:42:38,220
square root.

601
00:42:38,220 --> 00:42:43,700
Another function that comes up all the time in deep learning is log sum x.

602
00:42:43,700 --> 00:42:47,220
You see this in things like the cross entropy function.

603
00:42:47,220 --> 00:42:52,940
The naive way of implementing this is if you have an array, you apply x to the array, then

604
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:57,920
you sum out the x values, and then you take the log at the end.

605
00:42:57,920 --> 00:43:02,040
The failure modes to this are those two different ways that can go wrong.

606
00:43:02,040 --> 00:43:07,680
One is if any of your entries in the array is big, your x can overflow.

607
00:43:07,680 --> 00:43:15,560
So you just take x of some large number in float 32, a value of 90 would do this to you.

608
00:43:15,560 --> 00:43:16,560
You overflow.

609
00:43:16,560 --> 00:43:18,360
Now you've got an inf here.

610
00:43:18,360 --> 00:43:22,520
When you start doing arithmetic on the inf, it's probably going to turn into a noun pretty

611
00:43:23,300 --> 00:43:25,300
soon.

612
00:43:25,300 --> 00:43:26,300
That one's relatively obvious.

613
00:43:26,300 --> 00:43:29,340
A lot of people realize that that one's going to happen.

614
00:43:29,340 --> 00:43:35,480
Another case that this can fail is if all of the entries in the array are very negative,

615
00:43:35,480 --> 00:43:38,180
then all of the x's underflow to zero.

616
00:43:38,180 --> 00:43:42,820
Individually, each of these mistakes in the x is not a big deal.

617
00:43:42,820 --> 00:43:45,980
X of a very negative number is some small number epsilon.

618
00:43:45,980 --> 00:43:50,140
You round that to zero, your error is only order of epsilon.

619
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:56,040
The problem is when you sum up all the zeros, if all of them are literally zero, then the

620
00:43:56,040 --> 00:43:58,080
sum is also literally zero.

621
00:43:58,080 --> 00:43:59,920
And then you pass that to a log.

622
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:07,280
So you get a negative int at the end, even if that's not algebraically the correct answer.

623
00:44:07,280 --> 00:44:11,520
So the good news is there's a relatively simple trick that lets us fix this.

624
00:44:11,520 --> 00:44:16,080
The way it works is we take the maximum of the array, and then we subtract the maximum

625
00:44:16,080 --> 00:44:18,480
off all of the entries.

626
00:44:18,480 --> 00:44:24,580
Then we compute log sum x of this new safe array that had the max subtracted off of it.

627
00:44:24,580 --> 00:44:29,340
And we add the max back on to the result after we take the log.

628
00:44:29,340 --> 00:44:34,140
In TensorFlow, there's a build-in version that does this for you, tf.reduce log sum

629
00:44:34,140 --> 00:44:35,460
x.

630
00:44:35,460 --> 00:44:37,980
You do need to know to use that, though.

631
00:44:37,980 --> 00:44:41,100
Someone was saying, don't all the deep learning frameworks already solve this?

632
00:44:41,100 --> 00:44:45,740
Well, a lot of them provide solutions to it, but you have to know to use those solutions.

633
00:44:45,740 --> 00:44:49,360
So whenever you see yourself doing a log sum x, be sure to use the stable variant of

634
00:44:49,360 --> 00:44:51,880
it.

635
00:44:51,880 --> 00:44:55,800
If we look at why this works, first off, we need to make sure that we're actually computing

636
00:44:55,800 --> 00:44:57,080
the value that we want.

637
00:44:57,080 --> 00:45:01,640
We want to see that the new expression is algebraically equivalent to the old one.

638
00:45:01,640 --> 00:45:05,840
So the way that we can see this is we've got this variable m representing the max.

639
00:45:05,840 --> 00:45:08,920
We've got this vector a representing the array.

640
00:45:08,920 --> 00:45:15,220
The new value that we're computing is m plus log sum over i of x of ai minus m.

641
00:45:15,220 --> 00:45:23,500
Well, each of these x terms, we can use the fact that x of ai minus m turns into x of

642
00:45:23,500 --> 00:45:25,780
ai divided by x of m.

643
00:45:25,780 --> 00:45:30,060
And so we've simplified these terms inside the sum.

644
00:45:30,060 --> 00:45:37,900
Now it turns out that every term in the sum has this factor division by x of m, and this

645
00:45:37,900 --> 00:45:40,240
factor doesn't depend on ai at all.

646
00:45:40,240 --> 00:45:42,960
So we can actually pull it outside the summation.

647
00:45:42,960 --> 00:45:48,880
So now we've got this 1 over x m factor multiplied by this summation factor.

648
00:45:48,880 --> 00:45:56,600
Well, a logarithm of two factors can be split into the sum of the logarithm of both those

649
00:45:56,600 --> 00:45:58,600
factors.

650
00:45:58,600 --> 00:46:03,640
The logarithm of 1 over x is actually just negative log of x.

651
00:46:03,640 --> 00:46:08,500
We get m minus log x m plus the sum over all these factors.

652
00:46:08,500 --> 00:46:11,980
This log x, obviously, that turns into the identity.

653
00:46:11,980 --> 00:46:17,220
So we get m minus m, this cancels, and oh, I actually have a mistake.

654
00:46:17,220 --> 00:46:21,100
I left out the log here, but there should be a log right there.

655
00:46:21,100 --> 00:46:28,700
So we've actually recovered the original version of the expression that we were trying to optimize.

656
00:46:28,700 --> 00:46:33,380
So the other thing we need to check to see that this works is we need to make sure that

657
00:46:33,880 --> 00:46:36,680
the two failure modes we identified earlier are gone.

658
00:46:36,680 --> 00:46:40,600
So we notice that there is a way that we can overflow the original version.

659
00:46:40,600 --> 00:46:44,280
We've also noticed that there was a way we could underflow every term of the original

660
00:46:44,280 --> 00:46:47,960
version and then get a negative int when we pass it to the log.

661
00:46:47,960 --> 00:46:50,540
So first let's look at the overflow case.

662
00:46:50,540 --> 00:46:56,480
Because we subtract off the max, all of the entries of safe array are at most zero.

663
00:46:56,480 --> 00:47:00,120
X of zero is just one, so we can compute that without any overflow.

664
00:47:00,120 --> 00:47:03,160
So we've eliminated the overflow problem.

665
00:47:03,260 --> 00:47:07,500
There was the case where every value of the x underflows.

666
00:47:07,500 --> 00:47:11,740
Well, we can still have a problem where some of the x terms underflow.

667
00:47:11,740 --> 00:47:17,740
The good news is subtracting off the max actually means that there has to be at least one entry

668
00:47:17,740 --> 00:47:20,180
of safe array that's equal to zero.

669
00:47:20,180 --> 00:47:23,380
Any entries that were tied for the max now have a value of zero.

670
00:47:23,380 --> 00:47:25,780
And so those are not going to underflow when we take the x.

671
00:47:25,780 --> 00:47:30,940
They'll get a value of one, and that's a nice stable value that can be computed easily.

672
00:47:30,960 --> 00:47:35,760
That means that the sum is now at least one, and we can safely pass that sum to the logarithm.

673
00:47:37,920 --> 00:47:42,240
So someone is asking if there's any book or blog summarizing all these numerical computation

674
00:47:42,240 --> 00:47:44,680
problems in the context of deep learning.

675
00:47:44,680 --> 00:47:49,720
There are definitely books about numerical computation.

676
00:47:49,720 --> 00:47:55,920
A lot of them don't really focus specifically on the problems that come up a lot in deep learning.

677
00:47:55,920 --> 00:48:00,360
And I don't actually know of one that is focused in that way.

678
00:48:00,360 --> 00:48:04,620
I will probably write an article about it or add it to the second edition of the deep

679
00:48:04,620 --> 00:48:05,780
learning textbook.

680
00:48:05,780 --> 00:48:12,740
But I don't know of a good resource to point you to yet other than this lecture.

681
00:48:12,740 --> 00:48:15,140
The softmax function comes up all the time in deep learning.

682
00:48:15,140 --> 00:48:23,700
We use it as the output of most neural nets for classification, for generating text, for

683
00:48:23,700 --> 00:48:28,860
selecting discrete actions in reinforcement learning agents.

684
00:48:28,880 --> 00:48:33,600
The main advice for the softmax is that you should use your library's buildinsoftmax function.

685
00:48:33,600 --> 00:48:38,640
If you try to make your own, it's easy to mess up and get a numerically unstable version.

686
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:42,680
If you do build your own, the way that you should do it is you should take the argument

687
00:48:42,680 --> 00:48:47,680
to the softmax, the logits, and you should track off the maximum value before you pass

688
00:48:47,680 --> 00:48:49,760
it through the softmax.

689
00:48:49,760 --> 00:48:52,840
The reason this works is very similar to the log sum x.

690
00:48:52,860 --> 00:48:59,180
I won't walk through exactly what all of the justification for it is, but it's basically

691
00:48:59,180 --> 00:49:02,980
the same logic as what we just saw with log sum x.

692
00:49:02,980 --> 00:49:06,340
The sigmoid function is also very common in deep learning.

693
00:49:06,340 --> 00:49:09,220
And it's essentially the same thing as softmax.

694
00:49:09,220 --> 00:49:14,100
It's basically a softmax where one of the inputs is hard coded to zero, and you pass

695
00:49:14,100 --> 00:49:17,020
it out just one of the outputs.

696
00:49:17,020 --> 00:49:21,220
So once again, the recommendation is to use your library's buildin sigmoid function.

697
00:49:21,220 --> 00:49:27,560
If you don't, you can stabilize it using the same trick as with the softmax.

698
00:49:27,560 --> 00:49:33,280
The cross entropy function is the loss for most neural nets that we train, especially

699
00:49:33,280 --> 00:49:35,800
if you have a softmax output.

700
00:49:35,800 --> 00:49:38,440
Everything that I say here also applies with sigmoid.

701
00:49:38,440 --> 00:49:42,280
Just replace the word softmax with sigmoid.

702
00:49:42,280 --> 00:49:46,880
This is probably one of the most common things that I see people screw up in deep learning

703
00:49:46,900 --> 00:49:53,020
is they implement their own cross entropy function, and they compute it using the probabilities.

704
00:49:53,020 --> 00:49:57,460
They actually literally apply the softmax and then compute the loss.

705
00:49:57,460 --> 00:50:03,100
What you actually want to do is use the logits, the values output by the neural net before

706
00:50:03,100 --> 00:50:04,980
you get to the softmax.

707
00:50:04,980 --> 00:50:08,700
And you want to compute your loss in terms of those.

708
00:50:08,700 --> 00:50:13,500
The reason is that the probabilities will lose gradient when the softmax saturates.

709
00:50:13,520 --> 00:50:17,840
The cross entropy function won't saturate unless you're already getting the right answer,

710
00:50:17,840 --> 00:50:21,920
and then you don't need any gradient because you're already winning.

711
00:50:21,920 --> 00:50:27,960
The short recommendation for how to write good code is to use a numerically stable version

712
00:50:27,960 --> 00:50:32,880
of the cross entropy function that's already provided to you by your library.

713
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:38,840
If you're using TensorFlow, you can use tf.nn.softmax cross entropy with logits.

714
00:50:38,840 --> 00:50:42,600
If you're using another software framework, you need to be very careful and check that

715
00:50:42,660 --> 00:50:48,300
it actually is computing the cross entropy using the logits rather than the probabilities.

716
00:50:48,300 --> 00:50:53,500
A lot of frameworks screw this up and use the output of the softmax rather than the

717
00:50:53,500 --> 00:50:54,500
input to the softmax.

718
00:50:54,500 --> 00:50:59,940
It's a common enough problem that I've actually seen this in frameworks that I've used.

719
00:50:59,940 --> 00:51:04,140
If you do make your own cross entropy function, you should use the stabilization tricks for

720
00:51:04,140 --> 00:51:09,900
the softmax and the log sum x that come up inside this loss function in order to stabilize

721
00:51:09,900 --> 00:51:12,580
it.

722
00:51:12,580 --> 00:51:17,320
In general, if you have some bugs that you're encountering, there are a few really simple

723
00:51:17,320 --> 00:51:20,520
strategies that you can follow to catch them.

724
00:51:20,520 --> 00:51:25,280
One thing is that if you increase the learning rate for your gradient descent algorithm and

725
00:51:25,280 --> 00:51:29,400
the loss function value gets stuck, that for a very large learning rate, you find that

726
00:51:29,400 --> 00:51:34,240
your loss is flat over time as you take more steps.

727
00:51:34,240 --> 00:51:39,040
That probably means that the gradient is getting routed down to zero somewhere in your graph.

728
00:51:39,040 --> 00:51:44,260
You probably are losing numerical precision and ending up without a gradient.

729
00:51:44,260 --> 00:51:48,940
Otherwise, a large learning rate should make you move faster and faster and actually cause

730
00:51:48,940 --> 00:51:51,420
explosion rather than getting stuck.

731
00:51:51,420 --> 00:51:56,160
The most common way that I've seen this happen is when the cross entropy loss is calculated

732
00:51:56,160 --> 00:51:58,620
using probabilities instead of logits.

733
00:51:58,620 --> 00:52:04,540
If the softmax rounds a probability to one or zero, then the gradient through it is gone

734
00:52:04,540 --> 00:52:06,820
and you can't do very much about it.

735
00:52:06,820 --> 00:52:10,720
A lot of the time, people that encounter this problem with extreme values of the softmax

736
00:52:10,720 --> 00:52:15,880
will hack around it by clipping the value of the softmax somehow or other to avoid extreme

737
00:52:15,880 --> 00:52:16,960
values.

738
00:52:16,960 --> 00:52:20,640
That will make sure that you don't get nons in the forward prop when you take a log of

739
00:52:20,640 --> 00:52:24,880
the softmax, but it will also mean that you get zeros in the back prop when you try to

740
00:52:24,880 --> 00:52:26,880
compute the gradients.

741
00:52:26,880 --> 00:52:28,720
That's a really common bug.

742
00:52:28,720 --> 00:52:33,720
It's pretty easy to identify by the learning curve being flat.

743
00:52:33,780 --> 00:52:39,220
I've actually seen this a lot in third-party open-source implementations of generative

744
00:52:39,220 --> 00:52:41,500
adversarial nodes.

745
00:52:41,500 --> 00:52:45,100
This is one of those cases where it will look like you don't have a bug because if you use

746
00:52:45,100 --> 00:52:50,220
a small enough learning rate, your code will basically work, but it just won't perform

747
00:52:50,220 --> 00:52:52,140
quite as well as it ought to.

748
00:52:52,140 --> 00:52:56,260
If you use a large learning rate, then it will break and it will look like the algorithm

749
00:52:56,260 --> 00:53:03,260
is not robust to hyperparameters, but really the problem is that your code is not numerically

750
00:53:03,280 --> 00:53:08,280
precise enough to handle some of the larger learning rates.

751
00:53:08,280 --> 00:53:12,560
Another one of the bug hunting strategies I use is just if you see an explosion, immediately

752
00:53:12,560 --> 00:53:18,160
become very suspicious of anywhere that you see a log x square root or division in your

753
00:53:18,160 --> 00:53:22,160
code and just look and see if you're doing a divide by zero, a square root of a negative

754
00:53:22,160 --> 00:53:24,640
number and x of a large number and so on.

755
00:53:24,640 --> 00:53:29,840
Also, you should always suspect the code that has changed the most recently.

756
00:53:29,860 --> 00:53:33,860
For example, if you were running your code and everything's fine and then eventually

757
00:53:33,860 --> 00:53:39,420
you add something like batch norm and suddenly you see explosion, then you should probably

758
00:53:39,420 --> 00:53:44,100
suspect that maybe the problem is when you divide by the square root of the variance

759
00:53:44,100 --> 00:53:49,180
in your batch norm, and that's very likely to be where the problem is.

760
00:53:49,180 --> 00:53:54,380
A lot of the time I find that when I'm helping people like interns or brain residents, just

761
00:53:54,380 --> 00:53:59,500
following this bug hunting strategy with my colleagues at Google Brain will help me almost

762
00:53:59,560 --> 00:54:04,560
immediately solve problems that were very mysterious for someone who is new to deep learning.

763
00:54:04,560 --> 00:54:07,560
And if you can learn this pattern, it can accelerate your workflow quite a lot.

764
00:54:07,560 --> 00:54:08,560
Okay, thanks.

765
00:54:08,560 --> 00:54:09,560
Glad you enjoyed the talk.

